(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["chandogya"]={"slug":"chandogya","name":"Chāndogya","deva":"छान्दोग्य","note":"","intro":["A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Sāmaveda (Kauthuma–Rāṇāyanīya school), forming the last eight prapāṭhakas of the Chāndogya Brāhmaṇa. With the Bṛhadāraṇyaka it is the largest and one of the oldest of the major Upaniṣads, and a primary source-text for all schools of Vedānta. The name comes from chandoga — a singer of the chandas (the Sāmans). It contains the most celebrated teachings of the Vedānta: the Udgītha-vidyā, the Śāṇḍilya-vidyā, the Pañcāgni-vidyā, the Sad-vidyā with tat tvam asi, the Bhūmā-vidyā, and the Dahara-vidyā.","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (chhaandogya.itx). Cross-checked against Śaṅkara's Chāndogyopaniṣad-bhāṣya. The original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Chāndogya Upaniṣad","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["छान्दोग्य उपनिषत् — \"The Upaniṣad of the Chandogas (Sāman-singers)\"","A principal (mukhya) Upaniṣad of the Sāmaveda (Kauthuma–Rāṇāyanīya school), forming the last eight prapāṭhakas of the Chāndogya Brāhmaṇa. With the Bṛhadāraṇyaka it is the largest and one of the oldest of the major Upaniṣads, and a primary source-text for all schools of Vedānta. The name comes from chandoga — a singer of the chandas (the Sāmans). It contains the most celebrated teachings of the Vedānta: the Udgītha-vidyā, the Śāṇḍilya-vidyā, the Pañcāgni-vidyā, the Sad-vidyā with tat tvam asi, the Bhūmā-vidyā, and the Dahara-vidyā.","Sanskrit text source: sanskritdocuments.org (chhaandogya.itx). Cross-checked against Śaṅkara's Chāndogyopaniṣad-bhāṣya. The original text is in the public domain; all transliteration, glosses, translations, and commentary digests below are this compiler's own work."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Structure of the whole Upaniṣad (8 prapāṭhakas)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Prapāṭhaka</td><td>Khaṇḍas</td><td>Common name</td><td>Core subject</td></tr><tr><td>I</td><td>13</td><td>Udgītha-vidyā</td><td>Meditations on Oṃ / the Udgītha as essence, breath, sun, and the golden Person.</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>24</td><td>Sāma-upāsana</td><td>Meditations on the Sāman in its fivefold/sevenfold forms and in nature.</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>19</td><td>Madhu-, Gāyatrī-, Śāṇḍilya-vidyā</td><td>The honey of the Vedas; \"all this is Brahman\"; sarvaṃ khalv idaṃ brahma.</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>17</td><td>Saṃvarga-vidyā; Satyakāma; Upakosala</td><td>Wind/breath as the \"absorber\"; the fires teach; the teacher's role.</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>24</td><td>Prāṇa-saṃvāda; Pañcāgni-vidyā; Vaiśvānara</td><td>The contest of the breaths; the five fires &amp; rebirth; the universal Self.</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>16</td><td>Sad-vidyā (Uddālaka &amp; Śvetaketu)</td><td>\"Being alone was, one only\"; the nine teachings of तत् त्वम् असि — <em>tat tvam asi</em>.</td></tr><tr><td>VII</td><td>26</td><td>Bhūmā-vidyā (Nārada &amp; Sanatkumāra)</td><td>The graded ascent from \"name\" to the Infinite (<em>bhūman</em>).</td></tr><tr><td>VIII</td><td>15</td><td>Dahara-vidyā; Prajāpati &amp; Indra</td><td>The space in the heart; the four-fold search for the true Self.</td></tr></table>","This document is being built prapāṭhaka by prapāṭhaka. This instalment covers Prapāṭhaka I (the Udgītha-vidyā); the remaining seven follow, appended to this same file."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The five commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["1. Ādi Śaṅkara (c. 700 CE) — Chāndogyopaniṣad-bhāṣya, the foundational Advaita commentary and the principal lens here. Distinguishes sharply between upāsanā (symbol-meditation, yielding prosperity and krama-mukti) — which dominates Prapāṭhakas I–V — and the direct brahma-vidyā of Prapāṭhaka VI (tat tvam asi). Reads the Udgītha meditations as karma-aṅga and pratīka worship that purify and steady the seeker.","2. Madhvācārya / Ānanda Tīrtha (13th c.) — Chāndogyopaniṣad-bhāṣya, the Dvaita reading. Oṃ, the Udgītha, the golden Person and the indwelling breath all denote Viṣṇu-Hari; the chief breath (mukhya-prāṇa) is Vāyu, the Lord's first and highest agent. Meditation is worship of the independent Lord through His symbols; jīva and Lord remain eternally distinct.","3. Rangarāmānuja (17th c.) — Chāndogya-prakāśikā, the Viśiṣṭādvaita reading. The symbols of meditation point to Nārāyaṇa as antaryāmin; the golden Person in the sun and the person in the eye are the Lord; the meditations worship His vibhūti (cosmic glory), with the released soul attaining likeness to Him.","4. The Advaita-Ashrama reading-tradition (Swami Gambhīrānanda's standard literal rendering &amp; class-notes; here in this compiler's paraphrase) — the careful translator's voice: settles the anvaya, the dense Sāmavedic ritual vocabulary (prastāva, udgītha, pratihāra, stobha), and the etymologies (sā + ama = sāman; ud-gī-tha; udita).","5. Sri Aurobindo (early 20th c.; The Upanishads and related essays; here in this compiler's paraphrase) — the integral / psychological reading. Oṃ is the sound-seed of the worlds; the prāṇa is the master-energy of life; the Udgītha meditations are disciplines for awakening the powers of the Word and breath; the golden Person is the Divine in the Sun of Truth.","On usage: under each khaṇḍa, Śaṅkara gives the Advaita construal (and the upāsanā / vidyā distinction); Madhva the Dvaita (Viṣṇu/Vāyu) reading; Rangarāmānuja the Viśiṣṭādvaita; Gambhīrānanda-tradition the literal settling of sense and ritual terms; Aurobindo the psychological angle. Digests are in this compiler's words, not verbatim quotations."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha (Sāmavedic peace invocation)","title":"","sa":["ॐ आप्यायन्तु ममाङ्गानि वाक्प्राणश्चक्षुः श्रोत्रमथो बलमिन्द्रियाणि च सर्वाणि ।","सर्वं ब्रह्मौपनिषदं माहं ब्रह्म निराकुर्यां मा मा ब्रह्म निराकरोत्","अनिराकरणमस्त्वनिराकरणं मेऽस्तु ।","तदात्मनि निरते य उपनिषत्सु धर्मास्ते मयि सन्तु ते मयि सन्तु ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ āpyāyantu mamāṅgāni vāk prāṇaś cakṣuḥ śrotram atho balam indriyāṇi ca sarvāṇi |","sarvaṃ brahmaupaniṣadam | māhaṃ brahma nirākuryāṃ mā mā brahma nirākarot |","anirākaraṇam astv anirākaraṇaṃ me 'stu |","tad ātmani nirate ya upaniṣatsu dharmās te mayi santu te mayi santu | oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[["āpyāyantu mama aṅgāni","3 pl. impv. + n. nom. pl.","may my limbs grow strong/full"],["vāk prāṇaḥ cakṣuḥ śrotram","nom.","speech, breath, eye, ear"],["atho balam indriyāṇi ca sarvāṇi","nom.","and strength, and all the senses"],["sarvam brahma-aupaniṣadam","n. nom.","all (this) is the Brahman of the Upaniṣads"],["mā ahaṃ brahma nirākuryām","prohib. opt.","may I never deny Brahman"],["mā mā brahma nirākarot","prohib. aor.","may Brahman never deny me"],["anirākaraṇam astu","n. nom. + impv.","let there be non-denial"],["tat ātmani nirate","m. loc. sg.","in (me) who delights in the Self"],["upaniṣatsu dharmāḥ","f. loc. pl. + m. nom.","the virtues (taught) in the Upaniṣads"],["te mayi santu","3 pl. impv.","may they dwell in me"]],"lit":"\"Oṃ. May my limbs grow vigorous — my speech, breath, eye, ear, and strength, and all my senses. All is the Brahman of the Upaniṣads. May I never deny Brahman; may Brahman never deny me. Let there be no denial; let there be no denial on my part. May the virtues that are (declared) in the Upaniṣads dwell in me who delights in the Self — may they dwell in me. Oṃ, peace, peace, peace.\"","para":"The Sāmavedic opening asks for the invigoration of every organ (the instruments of study and chant), affirms that all reality is the Brahman of the Upaniṣads, and prays for an unbroken, mutual non-rejection between the seeker and Brahman — that the Upaniṣadic virtues take root in one devoted to the Self.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the prayer for vigorous organs is apt for a text built on upāsanā, which requires steady instruments; sarvaṃ brahmaupaniṣadam sets the non-dual horizon the whole text serves."],["Madhva","the strengthening is sought from Hari, sustainer of the organs; \"non-denial\" is the soul's fidelity to the Lord and the Lord's protective grace."],["Rangarāmānuja","the senses are invigorated by the antaryāmin; the mutual non-rejection is the bond of devotion between the soul and the indwelling Lord."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","glosses brahmaupaniṣadam as \"the Brahman known through the Upaniṣads,\" and nirākuryām/nirākarot as \"may I not reject / may It not reject.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","the invocation gathers the vital and sensory powers into fullness (āpyāyana) so that the consciousness can hold the truth the Upaniṣad will unfold."]],"notes":["Visual — the prayer's three asks","<table><tr><td>Ask</td><td>Content</td></tr><tr><td>Strengthen the instruments</td><td>limbs · speech · breath · eye · ear · strength · all senses</td></tr><tr><td>Affirm the ground</td><td>sarvaṃ brahmaupaniṣadam — all is the Upaniṣadic Brahman</td></tr><tr><td>Bind seeker &amp; Brahman</td><td>\"may I not deny Brahman; may Brahman not deny me\"; may the Upaniṣadic virtues abide in me</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Recite to \"tune\" the body-mind before chant or study: the prayer treats the senses as the instruments of knowledge that must first be made strong and whole.","•  \"May I never deny Brahman; may Brahman never deny me\" is a mutual-fidelity vow — a posture of non-rejection to carry through the long study ahead.","•  The triple śānti quiets the three disturbances (self, beings, cosmos) before the largest of the Upaniṣads begins."]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I — The Udgītha-vidyā","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"The Udgītha-vidyā","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The first chapter is a sustained meditation on Oṃ, called here the Udgītha (the loud-sung, central portion of the Sāman chanted by the Udgātṛ priest). Across thirteen khaṇḍas Oṃ is contemplated as the essence of all essences, as the unconquerable chief breath, as the Sun, as the imperishable into which the gods escaped death, and as the golden Person. Śaṅkara's frame: these are upāsanās (symbol-worships) — they yield prosperity and a gradual path, preparing the seeker for the direct knowledge of Prapāṭhaka VI."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 1 (1.1)","title":"Oṃ, the essence of all essences","sa":["ॐ इत्येतदक्षरमुद्गीथमुपासीत । ॐ इति ह्युद्गायति तस्योपव्याख्यानम् ॥ १ ॥","एषां भूतानां पृथिवी रसः पृथिव्या आपो रसः । अपामोषधयो रस","ओषधीनां पुरुषो रसः पुरुषस्य वाग्रसो वाच ऋग्रस ऋचः साम रसः","साम्न उद्गीथो रसः ॥ २ ॥","स एष रसानाꣳरसतमः परमः परार्ध्योऽष्टमो यदुद्गीथः ॥ ३ ॥","... वागेवर्क्प्राणः सामोमित्येतदक्षरमुद्गीथः । तद्वा एतन्मिथुनं","यद्वाक्च प्राणश्चर्क्च साम च ॥ ५ ॥","... आपयिता ह वै कामानां भवति य एतदेवं विद्वानक्षरमुद्गीथमुपास्ते ॥ ७ ॥","तद्वा एतदनुज्ञाक्षरं यद्धि किंचानुजानात्योमित्येव तदाह ... ॥ ८ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ ity etad akṣaram udgītham upāsīta | oṃ iti hy udgāyati tasyopavyākhyānam || 1 ||","eṣāṃ bhūtānāṃ pṛthivī rasaḥ | pṛthivyā āpo rasaḥ | apām oṣadhayo rasaḥ | oṣadhīnāṃ puruṣo rasaḥ | puruṣasya vāg rasaḥ | vāca ṛg rasaḥ | ṛcaḥ sāma rasaḥ | sāmna udgītho rasaḥ || 2 ||","sa eṣa rasānāṃ rasatamaḥ paramaḥ parārdhyo'ṣṭamo yad udgīthaḥ || 3 ||","vāg evark prāṇaḥ sāma | om ity etad akṣaram udgīthaḥ | tad vā etan mithunaṃ yad vāk ca prāṇaś cark ca sāma ca || 5 ||","āpayitā ha vai kāmānāṃ bhavati ya etad evaṃ vidvān akṣaram udgītham upāste || 7 ||"],"words":[["oṃ iti etat akṣaram","n. nom.","this syllable \"Oṃ\""],["udgītham upāsīta","m. acc. + 3 sg. opt.","one should meditate (on it) as the Udgītha"],["upavyākhyānam","n. nom. sg.","(this is) the explanation (of it)"],["rasaḥ","m. nom. sg.","essence, sap, quintessence"],["eṣām bhūtānām pṛthivī rasaḥ","gen. pl. + nom.","of these beings, earth is the essence"],["rasatamaḥ paramaḥ parārdhyaḥ","m. nom. superl.","the most essential, supreme, belonging to the highest half"],["aṣṭamaḥ yad udgīthaḥ","m. nom.","the eighth, namely the Udgītha"],["mithunam","n. nom. sg.","a (coupled) pair"],["vāk ca prāṇaḥ ca · ṛk ca sāma ca","nom.","speech & breath · ṛc & sāman"],["āpayitā kāmānām","m. nom. + gen. pl.","a fulfiller of desires"],["anujñā-akṣaram","n. nom.","the syllable of assent (\"yes\")"]],"lit":"\"One should meditate on this syllable Oṃ as the Udgītha, for one sings (the Udgītha) beginning with 'Oṃ.' Here is its explanation: of these beings the essence is the earth; of earth, water; of water, plants; of plants, man; of man, speech; of speech, the Ṛc; of the Ṛc, the Sāman; of the Sāman, the Udgītha. This Udgītha is the essence of (all) essences, the supreme, the highest, the eighth. … Speech is the Ṛc, breath is the Sāman, and this syllable Oṃ is the Udgītha; this is a pair — speech and breath, Ṛc and Sāman. … He who, knowing thus, meditates on the syllable Oṃ as the Udgītha becomes a fulfiller of desires. This is the syllable of assent, for whatever one assents to, one says just 'Oṃ.'\"","para":"Oṃ — the Udgītha — is approached through an ascending chain of essences (<em>rasa</em>): matter distils upward through earth, water, plants, man, speech, Ṛc, Sāman, to the Udgītha, \"the essence of all essences,\" the eighth and supreme. Oṃ also unites the great pairs (speech+breath, Ṛc+Sāman), and is the universal syllable of assent (\"yes\"). To meditate on it thus is to become a fulfiller of desires — and the text adds that what is done with knowledge, faith, and the secret meaning (vidyā, śraddhā, upaniṣad) is far more potent than what is done in ignorance.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a pratīka-upāsanā — Oṃ (a symbol) is meditated as the Udgītha and the essence of all; the fruit is kāma-āpti (fulfilment), a worldly/heavenly result, not liberation. The vidyā/avidyā coda establishes that knowledge-charged action is superior."],["Madhva","Oṃ and the Udgītha denote Viṣṇu, the very essence (rasa) of all things; the \"fulfiller of desires\" is the Lord, worshipped through the syllable; the ascending essences display His pervading presence."],["Rangarāmānuja","the Udgītha-Oṃ is the Lord's symbol; the chain of essences is His ordered immanence; meditation yields His grace and the fruits He bestows."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","fixes rasatama (\"the most quintessential\"), parārdhya (\"belonging to the higher half/most valuable\"), the \"eighth\" counting from earth, and anujñā-akṣara (\"syllable of permission/assent\")."],["Sri Aurobindo","the rasa-series is the Veda's vision of a single sap of being concentrating into the Word; Oṃ is that concentrated power of the Spirit, and meditation on it aligns the soul with the creative affirmation behind things."]],"notes":["Visual — the ladder of essences (rasa)","beings → EARTH → water → plants → man → speech → ṚC → SĀMAN → UDGĪTHA (Oṃ)","(each is the \"essence/sap\" of the one before)","▲","\"rasānāṃ rasatamaḥ\" — the essence of essences,","supreme · highest · the eighth","<table><tr><td>Pair (mithuna) united in Oṃ</td><td>members</td></tr><tr><td>speech ⟷ breath</td><td>vāk · prāṇa</td></tr><tr><td>Ṛc ⟷ Sāman</td><td>the verse &amp; its melody</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  Use the rasa-ladder as a contemplative ascent: trace the world \"distilling\" upward to Oṃ, training the mind to seek the essence within each layer rather than the surface.","•  Oṃ as the syllable of assent is a practical handle — meet reality with the \"yes\" of Oṃ; meditation here is the cultivation of that fundamental affirmation.","•  The text's own coda is the most usable point: what you do with knowledge + faith + the inner meaning is \"more powerful\" — bring all three to any practice, not mechanical repetition."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 2 (1.2)","title":"The unconquerable chief breath","sa":["देवासुरा ह वै यत्र संयेतिरे उभये प्राजापत्यास्तद्ध देवा उद्गीथमाजह्रुर्","अनेनैनानभिभविष्याम इति ॥ १ ॥","ते ह नासिक्यं प्राणमुद्गीथमुपासांचक्रिरे तꣳ हासुराः पाप्मना विविधुस्","तस्मात्तेनोभयं जिघ्रति सुरभि च दुर्गन्धि च पाप्मना ह्येष विद्धः ॥ २ ॥","... अथ ह य एवायं मुख्यः प्राणस्तमुद्गीथमुपासांचक्रिरे तꣳ हासुरा","ऋत्वा विदध्वंसुर्यथाश्मानमाखणमृत्वा विध्वꣳसेतैवम् ॥ ७ ॥","... नैवैतेन सुरभि न दुर्गन्धि विजानात्यपहतपाप्मा ह्येष ... ॥ ९ ॥"],"iast":["devāsurā ha vai yatra saṃyetire ubhaye prājāpatyāḥ | tad dha devā udgītham ājahrur anenainān abhibhaviṣyāma iti || 1 ||","te ha nāsikyaṃ prāṇam udgītham upāsāṃcakrire | taṃ hāsurāḥ pāpmanā vividhuḥ | tasmāt tenobhayaṃ jighrati surabhi ca durgandhi ca | pāpmanā hy eṣa viddhaḥ || 2 ||","atha ha ya evāyaṃ mukhyaḥ prāṇas tam udgītham upāsāṃcakrire | taṃ hāsurā ṛtvā vidadhvaṃsuḥ | yathāśmānam ākhaṇam ṛtvā vidhvaṃseta evam || 7 ||","naivaitena surabhi na durgandhi vijānāti | apahatapāpmā hy eṣa || 9 ||"],"words":[["devāsurāḥ saṃyetire","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. pf.","gods and demons strove together"],["ubhaye prājāpatyāḥ","pron. + m. nom. pl.","both descended from Prajāpati"],["udgītham ājahruḥ","m. acc. + 3 pl. pf.","took up the Udgītha (as their means)"],["anena enān abhibhaviṣyāmaḥ","instr. + acc. + 1 pl. fut.","\"by this we shall overcome them\""],["nāsikyaṃ prāṇam","m. acc. sg.","the breath in the nose (smell)"],["pāpmanā vividhuḥ","m. instr. + 3 pl. pf.","pierced (it) with evil"],["ubhayaṃ jighrati: surabhi ca durgandhi ca","n. acc.","smells both: the fragrant and the foul"],["mukhyaḥ prāṇaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the chief (mouth-)breath"],["ṛtvā vidadhvaṃsuḥ","ger. + 3 pl. pf.","striking against (it), were shattered"],["aśmānam ākhaṇam","m. acc. sg.","a solid rock / a clod-against-a-rock"],["apahata-pāpmā","m. nom. sg.","one whose evil is struck away (sinless)"]],"lit":"\"When the gods and demons — both descended from Prajāpati — strove together, the gods took up the Udgītha (thinking) 'by this we shall overcome them.' They meditated on the breath-in-the-nose as the Udgītha; the demons pierced it with evil — therefore with it one smells both the fragrant and the foul, for it is pierced by evil.\" (So too with speech, eye, ear, and mind: each smells/speaks/sees/hears/thinks both good and evil.) \"Then they meditated on the chief breath (in the mouth) as the Udgītha; striking against it the demons were shattered, as a clod is shattered striking a solid rock. … With it one does not perceive fragrant or foul, for it is free of evil.\"","para":"In the perennial war of gods and demons (the higher and lower impulses), the gods try to win by meditating on each sense-organ as the Udgītha — but the demons \"pierce each with evil,\" which is why every sense registers both good and bad (the senses are morally mixed). Only the chief breath (mukhya-prāṇa, the vital force behind the mouth) proves unpierceable: the demons shatter against it like a clod against rock. It alone is apahata-pāpmā, untouched by evil — and so the breath is the true Udgītha.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","gods and demons are the scripturally-ordained and the worldly tendencies in each person; the sense-organs are \"pierced\" because they function toward both good and evil; the mukhya-prāṇa alone is apahata-pāpmā and so the proper ālambana (support) of the Udgītha-meditation."],["Madhva","the chief breath is Vāyu / Mukhya-prāṇa, the Lord's highest agent and the chief of jīvas, intrinsically pure; the demons' defeat marks the supremacy of Hari's first servant; the sense-deities, being lower, are vulnerable."],["Rangarāmānuja","the indwelling Lord, as the chief breath, is untouched by the evil that taints the embodied senses; meditation on Him as prāṇa secures the worshipper."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies nāsikya prāṇa (the olfactory breath) vs. mukhya prāṇa (the principal vital force), ṛtvā (\"having struck against\"), and apahata-pāpmā (\"rid of sin\")."],["Sri Aurobindo","the battle is the eternal struggle of the divine and undivine powers in the evolving being; the senses are double-edged, but the central life-energy, rightly held, is invincible and pure — the key to victory in the inner war."]],"notes":["Visual — the gods' meditations and the demons' attack","<table><tr><td>Meditated as Udgītha</td><td>Demons' result</td><td>Why each sense is \"mixed\"</td></tr><tr><td>nose-breath (smell)</td><td>pierced by evil</td><td>smells both fragrant &amp; foul</td></tr><tr><td>speech</td><td>pierced by evil</td><td>speaks both truth &amp; falsehood</td></tr><tr><td>eye (sight)</td><td>pierced by evil</td><td>sees both fair &amp; foul</td></tr><tr><td>ear (hearing)</td><td>pierced by evil</td><td>hears both worthy &amp; unworthy</td></tr><tr><td>mind</td><td>pierced by evil</td><td>wills both right &amp; wrong</td></tr><tr><td>chief breath (mouth)</td><td>demons shattered (clod vs. rock)</td><td>free of evil (apahata-pāpmā)</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  The teaching diagnoses why the senses are unreliable guides: each is \"pierced\" — morally double. The breath alone is undivided; hence breath-centred meditation is the firm ground.","•  Identify your own \"demon-pierced\" faculties (where attention drifts to the foul as readily as the fair); the practice is to anchor in the one faculty that is not double — the living breath.","•  The \"clod against rock\" image is a meditation on invulnerability through the right support — what is rested on the chief breath cannot be shattered."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 3 (1.3)","title":"The Sun, the vyāna, and the syllables of the Udgītha","sa":["अथाधिदैवतं य एवासौ तपति तमुद्गीथमुपासीतोद्यन्वा एष प्रजाभ्य उद्गायति ।","उद्यꣳस्तमो भयमपहन्त्यपहन्ता ह वै भयस्य तमसो भवति य एवं वेद ॥ १ ॥","... अथ खलु व्यानमेवोद्गीथमुपासीत यद्वै प्राणिति स प्राणो यदपानिति सोऽपानः ।","अथ यः प्राणापानयोः संधिः स व्यानो यो व्यानः सा वाक् ... ॥ ३ ॥","अथ खलूद्गीथाक्षराण्युपासीतोद्गीथ इति प्राण एवोत्प्राणेन ह्युत्तिष्ठति","वाग्गीर्वाचो ह गिर इत्याचक्षतेऽन्नं थमन्ने हीदꣳ सर्वꣳ स्थितम् ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["athādhidaivataṃ ya evāsau tapati tam udgītham upāsīta | udyan vā eṣa prajābhya udgāyati | udyaṃs tamo bhayam apahanti | apahantā ha vai bhayasya tamaso bhavati ya evaṃ veda || 1 ||","atha khalu vyānam evodgītham upāsīta | yad vai prāṇiti sa prāṇaḥ | yad apāniti so'pānaḥ | atha yaḥ prāṇāpānayoḥ saṃdhiḥ sa vyānaḥ | yo vyānaḥ sā vāk || 3 ||","atha khalūdgīthākṣarāṇy upāsīta | \"ud-gī-tha\" iti | prāṇa evot, prāṇena hy uttiṣṭhati | vāg gīḥ, vāco ha gira ity ācakṣate | annaṃ tham, anne hīdaṃ sarvaṃ sthitam || 6 ||"],"words":[["adhidaivatam","n. acc. (adv.)","with reference to the deities (cosmic)"],["asau tapati","pron. + 3 sg.","yonder one (the sun) that gives heat"],["udyan prajābhyaḥ udgāyati","pres.part. + 3 sg.","rising, it sings-aloud for creatures"],["tamaḥ bhayam apahanti","n. acc. + 3 sg.","dispels darkness and fear"],["vyānaḥ","m. nom. sg.","the \"diffused\" breath (between in- and out-breath)"],["prāṇāpānayoḥ saṃdhiḥ","m. gen. du. + nom.","the junction of in-breath and out-breath"],["aprāṇann anapānan","pres.part. (neg.)","neither breathing in nor out"],["vācam abhivyāharati","f. acc. + 3 sg.","one utters speech (during vyāna)"],["ud-gī-tha","the three syllables","ud = prāṇa; gī = speech (gir); tha = food (anna)"]],"lit":"\"Now, with reference to the deities: one should meditate on yonder (sun) that gives heat as the Udgītha. Rising, it sings aloud for creatures; rising, it dispels darkness and fear. He who knows thus becomes a dispeller of fear and darkness. … Next, one should meditate on the vyāna as the Udgītha. What one breathes out is prāṇa; what one breathes in is apāna; the junction of in-breath and out-breath is vyāna — and vyāna is speech. Therefore one utters speech while neither breathing in nor out (i.e., in the held breath, vyāna). … Next, one should meditate on the syllables of 'ud-gī-tha': ud is breath (prāṇa), for by breath one rises up; gī is speech (gir); tha is food (anna), for in food all this is established.\"","para":"Three meditations on the Udgītha: (1) cosmic — the rising Sun that \"sings forth\" and banishes darkness and fear; (2) physiological — the vyāna, the breath held at the junction of in- and out-breath, in which speech (and all forceful acts) actually occur; (3) etymological — the three syllables ud-gī-tha decoded as breath–speech–food, then expanded onto the cosmos (heaven–mid-region–earth; sun–air–fire; Sāma-/Yajur-/Ṛg-veda). The khaṇḍa ends with the \"milking of speech\": the knower gains food and the eating of it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a set of distinct upāsanās on one symbol (sun / vyāna / the syllables), each with its own stated fruit; the vyāna meditation rests on the physiological fact that articulate speech requires the breath momentarily held."],["Madhva","the Sun and the breaths are forms and agents of Hari; vyāna as the support of speech and strength reflects the Lord's sustaining power; the syllable-meanings point to His threefold cosmic presence."],["Rangarāmānuja","the Sun-Udgītha is the Lord as the dispeller of the soul's darkness; the breath-meditations worship Him as the inner vital ruler."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","settles vyāna as the breath at the saṃdhi (junction), explains why speech occurs in it, and the etymological assignments ud/gī/tha → prāṇa/gir/anna."],["Sri Aurobindo","the Sun is the godhead of illumined Truth that drives out the night of ignorance and fear; vyāna is the pervading life-force; the syllabic meditation gathers breath, word and substance into the single power of the Udgītha."]],"notes":["Visual — the three Udgītha-meditations","<table><tr><td>Meditation</td><td>Identified as</td><td>Fruit</td></tr><tr><td>Sun (adhidaivata)</td><td>the rising \"singer\" that dispels darkness</td><td>dispeller of fear &amp; darkness</td></tr><tr><td>Vyāna (adhyātma)</td><td>the held breath at the prāṇa–apāna junction; speech &amp; all strong acts occur in it</td><td>strength in action</td></tr><tr><td>Syllables ud-gī-tha</td><td>ud = breath · gī = speech · tha = food</td><td>food &amp; its enjoyment</td></tr></table>","ud  →  prāṇa (breath)   →  heaven · sun · Sāma-veda","gī  →  gir / vāk (speech)→  mid-region · air · Yajur-veda","tha →  anna (food)       →  earth · fire · Ṛg-veda","Practical steps / observations","•  The vyāna meditation is a precise prāṇāyāma cue: notice that speech and exertion happen in the retained breath (neither inhaling nor exhaling) — a doorway to kumbhaka awareness.","•  The Sun-meditation pairs an outer fact (sunrise dispels darkness) with an inner aim (dispelling fear) — use sunrise as a daily contemplative trigger.","•  Decoding ud-gī-tha trains the habit of finding breath, speech, and sustenance encoded in a single word — the Sāmavedic art of layered meaning."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 4 (1.4)","title":"Into the imperishable, beyond death","sa":["ॐ इत्येतदक्षरमुद्गीथमुपासीतोमिति ह्युद्गायति तस्योपव्याख्यानम् ॥ १ ॥","देवा वै मृत्योर्बिभ्यतस्त्रयीं विद्यां प्राविशꣳस्ते छन्दोभिरच्छादयन्","यदेभिरच्छादयꣳस्तच्छन्दसां छन्दस्त्वम् ॥ २ ॥","... ते नु विदित्वोर्ध्वा ऋचः साम्नो यजुषः स्वरमेव प्राविशन् ॥ ३ ॥","यदा वा ऋचमाप्नोत्योमित्येवातिस्वरत्येवꣳ सामैवं यजुरेष उ स्वरो","यदेतदक्षरमेतदमृतमभयं तत्प्रविश्य देवा अमृता अभया अभवन् ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["oṃ ity etad akṣaram udgītham upāsīta | oṃ iti hy udgāyati tasyopavyākhyānam || 1 ||","devā vai mṛtyor bibhyatas trayīṃ vidyāṃ prāviśan | te chandobhir acchādayan | yad ebhir acchādayaṃs tac chandasāṃ chandastvam || 2 ||","te nu viditvordhvā ṛcaḥ sāmno yajuṣaḥ svaram eva prāviśan || 3 ||","yadā vā ṛcam āpnoty oṃ ity evātisvarati | evaṃ sāma, evaṃ yajuḥ | eṣa u svaro yad etad akṣaram etad amṛtam abhayam | tat praviśya devā amṛtā abhayā abhavan || 4 ||"],"words":[["mṛtyoḥ bibhyataḥ","m. abl. + pres.part. gen. pl.","fearing death"],["trayīṃ vidyāṃ prāviśan","f. acc. + 3 pl.","entered the threefold knowledge (the Vedas)"],["chandobhiḥ acchādayan","n. instr. pl. + 3 pl.","covered (themselves) with the metres"],["chandasāṃ chandastvam","n. gen. pl. + nom.","the \"metre-ness\" of the metres (why chandas = \"cover\")"],["matsyam udake iva","acc. + loc.","like a fish in water"],["svaram eva prāviśan","m. acc. + 3 pl.","entered the tone (the sounded Oṃ)"],["oṃ iti atisvarati","3 sg.","one sounds \"Oṃ\" aloud (at the close)"],["akṣaram amṛtam abhayam","n. nom.","the imperishable, immortal, fearless"]],"lit":"\"One should meditate on this syllable Oṃ as the Udgītha. … The gods, afraid of death, entered the threefold knowledge (the Vedas); they covered themselves with the metres — and because they covered (acchādayan) themselves with these, that is the 'metre-hood' of the metres (chandas = covering). Death saw them there, in the Ṛc, Sāman, and Yajus, as one sees a fish in water. Knowing this, they rose up out of the Ṛc, Sāman, and Yajus and entered the tone (svara, the sounded Oṃ). For when one finishes a Ṛc one sounds 'Oṃ' aloud — so too with a Sāman and a Yajus. This tone is that syllable, the immortal, the fearless. Entering it, the gods became immortal and fearless.\"","para":"Even the gods, \"fearing death,\" first hide in the Vedas — but the rituals and metres, being acts in time, do not finally hide them: Death spies them \"like a fish in water.\" So they rise beyond the words into Oṃ itself (the svara, the bare imperishable tone with which every recitation ends), and that — the akṣara, immortal and fearless — makes them deathless. The teaching: ritual is a cover, but the imperishable Oṃ is the true refuge from death.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the gods' \"fear of death\" is the karma-bound condition; the Vedas-as-ritual cannot transcend death, but Oṃ as the akṣara (the imperishable, here standing for Brahman) does — a clear step from karma toward the deathless symbol."],["Madhva","the imperishable Oṃ denotes Viṣṇu, the only refuge from death; the gods attain deathlessness by entering Him; the metres/rituals are subordinate means under the Lord."],["Rangarāmānuja","Oṃ is the name of the Lord, the immortal and fearless; taking refuge in Him (not merely in ritual forms) grants immortality."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","explains the wordplay chandas (\"metre\") ← chad (\"to cover\"), the \"fish in water\" simile (still findable), and svara = the sounded Oṃ that concludes recitation."],["Sri Aurobindo","the forms of knowledge are veils in which the soul can still be found by mortality; only by rising into the imperishable sound-symbol of the Eternal does the godhead in us become deathless and free of fear."]],"notes":["Visual — the escape from death","gods FEAR DEATH","│","hide in the Vedas (chandas = \"covering\"):  Ṛc · Sāman · Yajus","│   ✗  Death sees them \"like a fish in water\"","▼","rise OUT, into the SVARA — the bare sounded \"Oṃ\" at every close","│   = akṣara · AMṚTA (immortal) · ABHAYA (fearless)","▼","gods become immortal and fearless","Practical steps / observations","•  The lesson for practice: forms (even sacred ones) are coverings, not the goal; the imperishable Oṃ behind the forms is the refuge. Rest attention on the bare tone, not the words.","•  Note the ritual fact made into a teaching: every Ṛc/Sāman/Yajus is sealed with \"Oṃ.\" That recurring tone is pointed to as the deathless — listen for the Oṃ that ends everything you chant.","•  \"Like a fish in water\" — wherever you still have a locatable form, you are still \"seeable\" by death; the practice is to enter the formless tone."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 5 (1.5)","title":"Oṃ is the Udgītha and the Praṇava (sun & breath)","sa":["अथ खलु य उद्गीथः स प्रणवो यः प्रणवः स उद्गीथ इत्यसौ वा आदित्य","उद्गीथ एष प्रणव ओमिति ह्येष स्वरन्नेति ॥ १ ॥","एतमु एवाहमभ्यगासिषं तस्मान्मम त्वमेकोऽसीति ह कौषीतकिः पुत्रमुवाच ... ॥ २ ॥","अथाध्यात्मं य एवायं मुख्यः प्राणस्तमुद्गीथमुपासीतोमिति ह्येष स्वरन्नेति ॥ ३ ॥"],"iast":["atha khalu ya udgīthaḥ sa praṇavo yaḥ praṇavaḥ sa udgītha iti | asau vā āditya udgītha eṣa praṇavaḥ | oṃ iti hy eṣa svarann eti || 1 ||","etam u evāham abhyagāsiṣaṃ tasmān mama tvam eko'sīti ha kauṣītakiḥ putram uvāca | raśmīṃs tvaṃ paryāvartayād bahavo vai te bhaviṣyantīty adhidaivatam || 2 ||","athādhyātmaṃ ya evāyaṃ mukhyaḥ prāṇas tam udgītham upāsīta | oṃ iti hy eṣa svarann eti || 3 ||"],"words":[["udgīthaḥ … praṇavaḥ","m. nom.","the Udgītha (Sāmic) … the Praṇava (Oṃ; Ṛg/Yajus)"],["asau ādityaḥ","pron. + m. nom.","yonder sun"],["oṃ iti svaran eti","pres.part. + 3 sg.","(it) moves sounding \"Oṃ\""],["abhyagāsiṣam","1 sg. aor. of abhi-gai","I sang to / worshipped (it)"],["tvam ekaḥ asi","2 sg.","\"you are my only (son)\""],["raśmīn paryāvartayāt","m. acc. pl. + impv.","\"meditate on the (sun's) many rays\""],["mukhyaḥ prāṇaḥ","m. nom.","the chief breath"],["bahavaḥ te bhaviṣyanti","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl. fut.","\"many (sons) will be yours\""]],"lit":"\"The Udgītha is the Praṇava, and the Praṇava is the Udgītha. Yonder sun is the Udgītha and the Praṇava, for it moves sounding 'Oṃ.' 'I worshipped this one alone — therefore you are my only son,' said Kauṣītaki to his son; 'meditate on the (sun's) rays (as many), and many sons will be yours' — this is with reference to the deities. Now with reference to the body: the chief breath is the Udgītha, for it too moves sounding 'Oṃ.'\"","para":"The two names — Udgītha (the Sāman-singer's term) and Praṇava (the Ṛc/Yajus term) — are identified: both are Oṃ. Oṃ is then seen cosmically as the Sun (which \"moves sounding Oṃ\") and bodily as the chief breath (which likewise \"sounds Oṃ\"). Kauṣītaki's aside teaches a subtle point about meditation's fruit: he worshipped the sun as one and got one son; meditate on its many rays and gain many — the form of the meditation shapes its result.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","establishes the identity Udgītha = Praṇava = Oṃ across the Vedas; the sun and chief breath are its adhidaivata and adhyātma seats; Kauṣītaki's instruction illustrates that the result follows the mode of meditation."],["Madhva","Oṃ in sun and breath denotes Hari and His agent Vāyu; the \"many rays\" worship reflects the Lord's manifold glories; fruits come by His grace, proportioned to the devotee's contemplation."],["Rangarāmānuja","the one Lord named Oṃ presides in sun and breath as antaryāmin; meditation on His amplitude yields amplitude of result."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","settles the Udgītha/Praṇava equivalence, svarann eti (\"proceeds resonating Oṃ\"), and the Kauṣītaki anecdote's point about quantity of fruit tracking the meditation's form."],["Sri Aurobindo","the single sacred Sound is seen in the cosmic light (sun) and the individual life (breath); the seer's counsel is that the breadth of our inner vision determines the breadth of our realisation."]],"notes":["Visual — one Oṃ, two names, two seats","<table><tr><td>Name</td><td>Tradition</td><td>Seat (cosmic / bodily)</td></tr><tr><td>Udgītha</td><td>Sāmaveda (the sung portion)</td><td>the Sun — \"moves sounding Oṃ\"</td></tr><tr><td>Praṇava</td><td>Ṛc / Yajus</td><td>the chief breath — \"moves sounding Oṃ\"</td></tr></table>","Kauṣītaki's lesson: meditate on the one → gain the one; meditate on the many rays → gain the many. The shape of the upāsanā shapes its fruit.","Practical steps / observations","•  A unifying recognition: whatever your tradition's name — Udgītha, Praṇava, Oṃ — it is the one syllable; don't let nomenclature fragment the practice.","•  Kauṣītaki's point is a real caution about sakāma (desire-driven) meditation: you tend to get the form of what you contemplate — so contemplate amplitude if you seek amplitude.","•  Hear Oṃ in both seats — the rising sun \"sounding Oṃ\" outwardly, the breath \"sounding Oṃ\" inwardly — linking macrocosm and microcosm in one tone."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 6 (1.6)","title":"The golden Person in the Sun","sa":["इयमेवर्गग्निः साम तदेतदेतस्यामृच्यध्यूढꣳ साम तस्मादृच्यध्यूढꣳ","साम गीयत इयमेव साग्निरमस्तत्साम ॥ १ ॥","... अथ य एषोऽन्तरादित्ये हिरण्मयः पुरुषो दृश्यते हिरण्यश्मश्रुर्","हिरण्यकेश आप्रणखात्सर्व एव सुवर्णः ॥ ६ ॥","तस्य यथा कप्यासं पुण्डरीकमेवमक्षिणी तस्योदिति नाम","स एष सर्वेभ्यः पाप्मभ्य उदित उदेति ह वै सर्वेभ्यः पाप्मभ्यो य एवं वेद ॥ ७ ॥"],"iast":["iyam evark | agniḥ sāma | tad etad etasyām ṛcy adhyūḍhaṃ sāma | tasmād ṛcy adhyūḍhaṃ sāma gīyate | iyam eva sā, agnir amaḥ, tat sāma || 1 ||","atha ya eṣo'ntarāditye hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣo dṛśyate hiraṇyaśmaśrur hiraṇyakeśa āpraṇakhāt sarva eva suvarṇaḥ || 6 ||","tasya yathā kapyāsaṃ puṇḍarīkam evam akṣiṇī | tasyodit i nāma | sa eṣa sarvebhyaḥ pāpmabhya udita udeti ha vai sarvebhyaḥ pāpmabhyo ya evaṃ veda || 7 ||"],"words":[["iyam (pṛthivī) eva ṛk","f. nom.","this (earth) is the Ṛc"],["agniḥ sāma","m. + n. nom.","fire is the Sāman"],["ṛci adhyūḍham sāma","f. loc. + n. nom.","the Sāman is set/rests upon the Ṛc"],["sā + amaḥ = sāma","(etymology)","\"sā\" (she/earth) + \"ama\" (fire/that) = sāman"],["antar-āditye hiraṇmayaḥ puruṣaḥ","m. loc. + nom.","the golden Person within the sun"],["hiraṇya-śmaśruḥ · -keśaḥ","m. nom.","golden-bearded · golden-haired"],["ā-praṇakhāt sarvaḥ suvarṇaḥ","m. abl. + nom.","golden all over, to the very nail-tips"],["kapyāsaṃ puṇḍarīkam","n. nom.","a (red) lotus"],["tasya \"ud\" iti nāma","n. nom.","his name is \"Ud\" (the Risen)"],["sarvebhyaḥ pāpmabhyaḥ uditaḥ","m. abl. pl. + nom.","risen above all evils"]],"lit":"\"This (earth) is the Ṛc; fire is the Sāman; this Sāman rests upon this Ṛc, therefore the Sāman is sung as resting on the Ṛc: 'sā' is this (earth), 'ama' is fire — that is sā-ma.\" (So too: mid-region/air; heaven/sun; stars/moon; the sun's white light is the Ṛc, its dark-blue depth the Sāman.) \"Now, the golden Person seen within the sun — golden-bearded, golden-haired, golden to the very tips of his nails; his eyes like a red lotus; his name is 'Ud' (the Risen), for he has risen above all evils. He who knows thus rises above all evils.\"","para":"Every Sāman \"rests on\" a Ṛc as melody on words; the khaṇḍa pairs cosmic Ṛc/Sāman couples (earth/fire, mid-region/air, heaven/sun, stars/moon, the sun's bright surface / its dark depth) and even etymologises sāman as sā (she/earth) + ama (he/fire) — a cosmic marriage. It culminates in the vision of the golden Person in the Sun — radiant to his fingertips, lotus-eyed — whose name is Ud (\"the Risen\"), risen above all evil. To know him is to rise above all evil; he is lord of the worlds beyond the sun.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","a saguṇa meditation; the golden Person in the sun is Brahman-with-qualities for upāsanā, lord of the higher worlds; \"risen above all evils\" is the meditator's promised fruit. The sā+ama etymology is a contemplative device, not philology proper."],["Madhva","the golden Person is Nārāyaṇa/Hari literally present in the solar orb; his golden, flawless form and the name \"Ud\" affirm the Lord's supremacy over all evil; the jīva worships, never identifies."],["Rangarāmānuja","the hiraṇmaya puruṣa is the Lord Nārāyaṇa, the antaryāmin of the sun; meditation on His auspicious form (lotus eyes, golden body) is saguṇa upāsanā yielding nearness to Him."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","handles the famous kapyāsaṃ puṇḍarīkam (rendered \"like a lotus,\" the traditional gloss explaining kapyāsa as reddish/blossomed), the sā/ama split, and udita (\"risen, free from evil\")."],["Sri Aurobindo","the golden Person is the Divine in the Sun of Truth-consciousness; his unblemished radiance is the supramental being, and to \"rise above all evil\" is to ascend into that luminous selfhood."]],"notes":["Visual — the cosmic Ṛc/Sāman pairs, and the golden Person","<table><tr><td>Ṛc (sā, the rested-on)</td><td>Sāman (ama, the resting)</td></tr><tr><td>earth</td><td>fire</td></tr><tr><td>mid-region</td><td>air</td></tr><tr><td>heaven</td><td>sun</td></tr><tr><td>stars</td><td>moon</td></tr><tr><td>the sun's white radiance</td><td>the sun's dark-blue depth</td></tr></table>","☀  within the SUN  ☀","HIRAṆMAYA PURUṢA — golden beard, golden hair,","golden to the nail-tips, eyes like a red lotus","name = \"UD\"  (risen above ALL evils)","→ the knower likewise rises above all evils","→ lord of the worlds BEYOND the sun &amp; the gods' desires","Practical steps / observations","•  The Ṛc/Sāman pairing is a contemplative template: in every couple one member is the support (word/earth) and one the song (melody/fire) — train the eye to see the resting-and-rested structure in nature.","•  The golden Person is a meditation-image of the Self risen above evil; hold the vision of radiance \"to the very fingertips\" as an icon of total, undivided purity.","•  His name \"Ud\" (\"risen\") is the practical seed: meditate to rise above (not suppress) the evils — the knower \"rises\" as the sun rises."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 7 (1.7)","title":"The Person in the eye","sa":["अथाध्यात्मं वागेवर्क्प्राणः साम ... वागेव सा प्राणोऽमस्तत्साम ॥ १ ॥","... अथ य एषोऽन्तरक्षिणि पुरुषो दृश्यते सैवर्क्तत्साम तदुक्थं तद्यजुस्तद्ब्रह्म ...","... तद्य इमे वीणायां गायन्त्येतं ते गायन्ति तस्मात्ते धनसनयः ॥ ६ ॥"],"iast":["athādhyātmaṃ vāg evark, prāṇaḥ sāma | ... vāg eva sā, prāṇo'maḥ, tat sāma || 1 ||","atha ya eṣo'ntar-akṣiṇi puruṣo dṛśyate saivark, tat sāma, tad uktham, tad yajuḥ, tad brahma | tasyaitasya tad eva rūpaṃ yad amuṣya (golden Person's) rūpam || 5 ||","tad ya ime vīṇāyāṃ gāyanty etaṃ te gāyanti tasmāt te dhanasanayaḥ || 6 ||"],"words":[["adhyātmam","adv.","with reference to the body/self"],["vāk eva ṛk · prāṇaḥ sāma","nom.","speech is the Ṛc · breath is the Sāman"],["antar-akṣiṇi puruṣaḥ dṛśyate","m. loc. + nom. + 3 sg. pass.","the Person seen within the eye"],["sā eva ṛk, tat sāma, tat uktham, tat yajuḥ, tat brahma","nom.","he is the Ṛc, Sāman, Uktha, Yajus, Brahman"],["tat eva rūpaṃ yat amuṣya rūpam","n. nom.","his very form is the (golden) form of that one (in the sun)"],["vīṇāyāṃ gāyanti","f. loc. + 3 pl.","(those who) sing to the lute"],["dhana-sanayaḥ","m. nom. pl.","winners of wealth"]],"lit":"\"Now with reference to the body: speech is the Ṛc, breath is the Sāman… speech is 'sā,' breath is 'ama' — that is the Sāman. … The Person seen within the eye — he is the Ṛc, the Sāman, the Uktha, the Yajus, the Brahman. His very form is the form of that (golden Person in the sun); his joints are their joints, his name their name. He is lord of the worlds on this side of the sun and of human desires. Those who sing to the lute sing of him — therefore they are winners of wealth.\"","para":"Mirroring khaṇḍa 6 (cosmic) on the bodily plane: the Ṛc/Sāman pairs are now speech/breath, eye/self, ear/mind. Its climax is the Person in the eye — the little reflected person one sees in another's pupil — declared identical in form to the golden Person in the sun. He is all the Vedas and Brahman, lord of the worlds this side of the sun and of human desires. The knower who sings the Sāman \"sings to both\" — the cosmic and the bodily Person — and so gains both worlds and both kinds of desire.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the eye-Person is the same saguṇa Brahman as the sun-Person, viewed adhyātma; \"lord of this-side worlds and human desires\" marks the bodily counterpart; knowing both unites the adhidaivata and adhyātma meditations."],["Madhva","the Person in the eye is Hari as the indwelling witness (akṣi-puruṣa), identical in nature to the solar Nārāyaṇa; the soul reveres Him within, distinct from itself."],["Rangarāmānuja","the eye-Person is the antaryāmin in the individual, of one form with the Lord in the sun; meditation on Him secures both the higher and the human goods."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","clarifies the akṣi-puruṣa (the reflected person in the pupil), the equation with the solar Person (\"same form, joints, name\"), and dhana-sanayaḥ (\"acquirers of wealth\")."],["Sri Aurobindo","the Person in the eye is the indwelling Divine seen in the very seat of vision; identical with the Person in the Sun, it teaches that the God within and the God in the cosmos are one being seen from two sides."]],"notes":["Visual — cosmic and bodily Persons, one form","<table><tr><td></td><td>Khaṇḍa 6 (adhidaivata)</td><td>Khaṇḍa 7 (adhyātma)</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>the Sun</td><td>the eye</td></tr><tr><td>Person</td><td>golden Person in the sun</td><td>person seen in the eye</td></tr><tr><td>Lordship</td><td>worlds beyond the sun; gods' desires</td><td>worlds this side; human desires</td></tr><tr><td>Identity</td><td>—</td><td>same form, same joints, same name</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  The \"person in the eye\" is a striking, concrete meditation-object: the tiny reflected figure in the pupil is pointed to as identical to the cosmic golden Person — outer and inner are one form.","•  The knower \"sings to both\" — a practice of double intention: address the meditation simultaneously to the Self in the sun and the Self in the eye, gaining \"both worlds.\"","•  Note the wealth-motif (lute-singers \"win wealth\"): this is still sakāma upāsanā; the deeper, desireless knowledge waits for Prapāṭhaka VI."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 8 (1.8)","title":"The three masters; the goal of the Sāman","sa":["त्रयो होद्गीथे कुशला बभूवुः शिलकः शालावत्यश्चैकितायनो दाल्भ्यः","प्रवाहणो जैवलिरिति ... ॥ १ ॥","... का साम्नो गतिरिति स्वर इति होवाच स्वरस्य का गतिरिति प्राण इति होवाच","प्राणस्य का गतिरित्यन्नमिति होवाचान्नस्य का गतिरित्याप इति होवाच ॥ ४ ॥","अपां का गतिरित्यसौ लोक इति होवाचामुष्य लोकस्य का गतिरिति न","स्वर्गं लोकमिति नयेदिति होवाच ... ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["trayo hodgīthe kuśalā babhūvuḥ — śilakaḥ śālāvatyaś caikitāyano dālbhyaḥ pravāhaṇo jaivalir iti || 1 ||","kā sāmno gatir iti — \"svara\" iti hovāca | svarasya kā gatir iti — \"prāṇa\" iti hovāca | prāṇasya kā gatir iti — \"annam\" iti hovāca | annasya kā gatir iti — \"āpaḥ\" iti hovāca || 4 ||","apāṃ kā gatir iti — \"asau lokaḥ\" iti hovāca | amuṣya lokasya kā gatir iti — na svargaṃ lokam atināyed iti hovāca | svargaṃ vayaṃ lokaṃ sāmābhisaṃsthāpayāmaḥ, svargasaṃstāvaṃ hi sāma || 5 ||"],"words":[["trayaḥ udgīthe kuśalāḥ","m. nom. pl.","three skilled in the Udgītha"],["Śilaka Śālāvatya; Caikitāyana Dālbhya; Pravāhaṇa Jaivali","names","the three disputants"],["kā sāmnaḥ gatiḥ","f. nom. + gen.","what is the goal/resort of the Sāman?"],["svaraḥ → prāṇaḥ → annam → āpaḥ → asau lokaḥ","m. nom.","tone → breath → food → water → that (heavenly) world"],["na atināyet","3 sg. opt. (prohib.)","one should not lead (it) beyond"],["svargaṃ lokam abhisaṃsthāpayāmaḥ","m. acc. + 1 pl.","we establish (the Sāman) in the heavenly world"],["svarga-saṃstāvam","n. acc.","(the Sāman is) praised-as-heaven"],["apratiṣṭhitam te sāma","n. nom.","\"your Sāman is unestablished\""],["mūrdhā te vipatet","m. nom. + 3 sg. opt.","\"your head would fall\""]],"lit":"\"Three were skilled in the Udgītha: Śilaka Śālāvatya, Caikitāyana Dālbhya, and Pravāhaṇa Jaivali. (Pravāhaṇa let the two brāhmaṇas speak first.) Śilaka asked Dālbhya: 'What is the goal of the Sāman?' — 'The tone (svara),' he said. 'And of the tone?' — 'Breath.' 'Of breath?' — 'Food.' 'Of food?' — 'Water.' 'Of water?' — 'That (heavenly) world.' 'And of that world?' — 'One should not lead the Sāman beyond the heavenly world; we establish the Sāman in heaven, for the Sāman is praised as heaven.' Śilaka said to Dālbhya: 'Your Sāman is unestablished; if anyone now said your head would fall, it would fall.'\"","para":"Three udgītha-experts meet to \"talk Udgītha.\" The kṣatriya Pravāhaṇa Jaivali defers, letting the two brāhmaṇas speak. Śilaka quizzes Dālbhya on the goal (gati) of the Sāman, eliciting a chain — tone → breath → food → water → the heavenly world — but Dālbhya stops at heaven (\"don't take the Sāman beyond it\"). Śilaka faults this as apratiṣṭhita (rootless) and warns \"your head would fall.\" The stage is set for Pravāhaṇa (khaṇḍa 9) to give the true, deeper answer.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the dialogue exposes the limit of a meditation that terminates in svarga (a perishable result); Dālbhya's answer is \"unestablished\" because heaven is not the final ground — the Sāman must be traced to its ultimate support (next khaṇḍa)."],["Madhva","the regress of \"goals\" points beyond all intermediate supports to Hari as the final resort; stopping at heaven mistakes a dependent realm for the independent Lord."],["Rangarāmānuja","the chain seeks the ultimate pratiṣṭhā (ground), which is Brahman; heaven is only a station within the Lord's order, not the resting-place of the Sāman."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","identifies the three sages and the kṣatriya's deference, glosses gati (\"resort/support\"), apratiṣṭhita (\"without firm basis\"), and the conventional \"head will fall\" warning for flawed upāsanā."],["Sri Aurobindo","the seekers trace the sacred chant back through its supports toward its source; to halt at the heavens is to mistake a plane of being for the ultimate, which the dialogue presses on to find."]],"notes":["Visual — the chain of \"goals\" (gati) of the Sāman","SĀMAN → tone(svara) → breath(prāṇa) → food(anna) → water(āpaḥ) → \"that world\"(svarga)","│","Dālbhya STOPS here (\"don't go beyond heaven\")","│","Śilaka: \"unestablished! your head would fall\"","▼","(Pravāhaṇa's deeper answer → Khaṇḍa 9)","Practical steps / observations","•  A model of genuine enquiry: each answer is pressed with \"and what is its goal?\" — the discipline of not stopping at the first resting-place. (Compare the neti neti refusal to settle.)","•  The \"head will fall\" formula recurs through Prapāṭhaka I: meditating on a symbol without knowing its true ground is dangerous — half-knowledge is a liability, not a virtue.","•  Note the kṣatriya Pravāhaṇa's courteous deference yet superior knowledge — a recurring Chāndogya motif (royal sages often hold the deeper teaching)."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 9 (1.9)","title":"Space (ākāśa) as the final goal","sa":["अस्य लोकस्य का गतिरित्याकाश इति होवाच सर्वाणि ह वा इमानि भूतान्य्","आकाशादेव समुत्पद्यन्त आकाशं प्रत्यस्तं यन्त्याकाशो ह्येवैभ्यो ज्यायानाकाशः","परायणम् ॥ १ ॥","स एष परोवरीयानुद्गीथः स एषोऽनन्तः परोवरीयो हास्य भवति","परोवरीयसो ह लोकाञ्जयति य एतदेवं विद्वान्परोवरीयाꣳसमुद्गीथमुपास्ते ॥ २ ॥"],"iast":["asya lokasya kā gatir iti — \"ākāśa\" iti hovāca | sarvāṇi ha vā imāni bhūtāny ākāśād eva samutpadyante | ākāśaṃ pratyastaṃ yanti | ākāśo hy evaibhyo jyāyān | ākāśaḥ parāyaṇam || 1 ||","sa eṣa parovarīyān udgīthaḥ | sa eṣo'nantaḥ | parovarīyo hāsya bhavati parovarīyaso ha lokāñ jayati ya etad evaṃ vidvān parovarīyāṃsam udgītham upāste || 2 ||"],"words":[["asya lokasya kā gatiḥ","m. gen. + f. nom.","what is the goal of this world?"],["ākāśaḥ","m. nom. sg.","space / ether"],["ākāśāt eva samutpadyante","m. abl. + 3 pl.","arise from space alone"],["ākāśaṃ pratyastaṃ yanti","m. acc. + 3 pl.","return back into space"],["ākāśaḥ ebhyaḥ jyāyān","m. nom. + abl. + comp.","space is greater than these"],["parāyaṇam","n. nom. sg.","the final resort"],["parovarīyān udgīthaḥ","m. nom. comp.","the ever-higher / progressively-superior Udgītha"],["anantaḥ","m. nom. sg.","endless, infinite"],["lokān jayati","m. acc. pl. + 3 sg.","wins the worlds"]],"lit":"\"'And what is the goal of this world?' — 'Space (ākāśa),' he said. 'For all these beings arise from space alone, and return back into space; space is greater than they, space is the final resort.' This is the ever-higher Udgītha; this is the endless. He who, knowing thus, meditates on the ever-higher Udgītha becomes ever-higher; he wins ever-higher worlds.\"","para":"Pravāhaṇa supplies the answer Dālbhya could not: the final goal of \"this world\" — and so of the whole chain — is ākāśa (space/ether), from which all beings arise and into which they return, \"greater than all, the last resort.\" This is the parovarīyas (\"ever-higher / progressively superior\") and endless Udgītha; its knower himself becomes ever-higher and \"wins ever-higher worlds.\" (Ākāśa here functions as the nearest symbol of the all-pervading, all-originating Brahman.)","coms":[["Śaṅkara","ākāśa here is a pratīka for Brahman — the source and end of all, \"greater than all\"; the \"ever-higher, endless Udgītha\" is Brahman meditated through space; still saguṇa, but the highest such meditation in the chapter."],["Madhva","ākāśa signifies the all-pervading Viṣṇu (or the Lord's spatial vibhūti); all beings arise from and return to Him; the \"ever-higher\" worships His supremacy."],["Rangarāmānuja","the all-originating ākāśa is Brahman-Nārāyaṇa, the material-and-final ground of beings; meditation on Him as parovarīyas yields ever-superior attainment."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","glosses parovarīyas (\"higher and higher / ever more excellent\"), parāyaṇa (\"ultimate resort\"), and notes that ākāśa is named because it is the subtlest, most pervasive symbol of the source."],["Sri Aurobindo","ākāśa is the ether of being, the first self-extension of the Spirit from which the worlds emerge and into which they withdraw — meditation on it lifts the consciousness toward the limitless."]],"notes":["Visual — ākāśa as origin and end","<pre>        all beings  ──ARISE FROM──►  ĀKĀŚA (space)</pre>","▲                          │","<pre>             └────────RETURN INTO───────┘</pre>","\"greater than all · the final resort (parāyaṇam)\"","│","= the PAROVARĪYAS (ever-higher), ENDLESS Udgītha","│","knower → becomes ever-higher → wins ever-higher worlds","Practical steps / observations","•  Ākāśa is the Upaniṣad's first great pointer to the all-containing ground: meditate on space as that from which all arises and into which all subsides — the most accessible symbol of the infinite.","•  Parovarīyas (\"ever-higher\") names a meditation whose very nature is open-ended ascent — contemplate the Udgītha as having no ceiling, and become \"ever-higher.\"","•  Watch how the dialogue refused to stop at heaven and only rested at the all-pervading: a model for not settling the search prematurely."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 10 (1.10)","title":"Uṣasti Cākrāyaṇa and the leftover beans","sa":["मटचीहतेषु कुरुष्वाटिक्या सह जायया उषस्तिर्ह चाक्रायण इभ्यग्रामे","प्रद्राणक उवास ॥ १ ॥","स हेभ्यं कुल्माषान्खादन्तं बिभिक्षे ... एतेषां मे देहीति होवाच ... ॥ ३ ॥","... कामो म उदपानमिति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["maṭacīhateṣu kuruṣv āṭikyā saha jāyayoṣastir ha cākrāyaṇa ibhyagrāme pradrāṇaka uvāsa || 1 ||","sa hebhyaṃ kulmāṣān khādantaṃ bibhikṣe | ... \"eteṣāṃ me dehi\" iti hovāca | tān asmai pradadau | \"hantānupānam\" ity | \"ucchiṣṭaṃ vai me pītaṃ syāt\" iti hovāca || 2–3 ||","\"na svid ete'py ucchiṣṭā\" iti | \"na vā ajīviṣyam imān akhādan\" iti hovāca | \"kāmo ma udapānam\" iti || 4 ||"],"words":[["maṭacī-hateṣu kuruṣu","m. loc. pl.","among the Kurus, ruined by hailstorms (or a locust-like blight)"],["āṭikyā saha jāyayā","f. instr.","with his (young) wife Āṭikī"],["uṣastiḥ cākrāyaṇaḥ","m. nom.","Uṣasti, son of Cakra"],["ibhya-grāme pradrāṇakaḥ uvāsa","m. loc. + nom. + 3 sg. pf.","lived destitute in a rich man's (elephant-keeper's) village"],["kulmāṣān khādantam bibhikṣe","m. acc. + pres.part. + 3 sg.","begged from (one) eating beans"],["eteṣām me dehi","impv.","\"give me some of these\""],["anupānam","n. acc.","(and) water to drink"],["ucchiṣṭam me pītam syāt","n. nom. + opt.","\"the drink would be leftover (defiled) for me\""],["na ajīviṣyam imān akhādan","1 sg. cond.","\"I would not have lived without eating these\""],["kāmaḥ me udapānam","m. nom.","\"water I can get at will\""]],"lit":"\"Among the Kurus, ravaged by hailstorms, Uṣasti Cākrāyaṇa lived destitute, with his young wife Āṭikī, in a rich elephant-keeper's village. He begged from the man, who was eating beans (kulmāṣa). 'Give me some of these,' he said; the man gave them. 'Here is water to drink as well.' 'That drink would be leftover (defiled) for me,' said Uṣasti. 'But were not these beans also leftover?' 'I would not have lived without eating them; but water I can get at will.'\"","para":"A famine-narrative that frames the next teaching. The learned Uṣasti Cākrāyaṇa, reduced to begging in a hard season, eats a rich man's leftover beans to survive but refuses his leftover water — drawing a sharp line: food he must eat to live (necessity overrides the rule of purity), but water \"he can get anywhere\" (no necessity, so the purity-rule stands). He saves the surplus beans for his wife. The vignette paints the dignity and discrimination of the knower even in destitution — and sets up his demonstration of superior knowledge at the sacrifice (khaṇḍa 11).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the story illustrates āpad-dharma (conduct in extremity) — in a life-threatening famine the ordinary rule against eating another's leavings is suspended, but not where there is no such necessity (water); it also displays the knower's poise, validating the teaching he will give."],["Madhva","Uṣasti's discrimination reflects the devotee's fidelity to dharma under the Lord's testing; his survival and later vindication come by Hari's grace."],["Rangarāmānuja","the sage's careful observance even in distress models the devotee's disciplined dependence on the Lord, who preserves and later honours him."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","explains maṭacī-hata (devastated, by hail or a vermin-blight), kulmāṣa (cooked beans/grains), pradrāṇaka (destitute/famished), and the ucchiṣṭa (leftover) purity-distinction."],["Sri Aurobindo","the episode shows the man of knowledge holding his inner law amid outer ruin — bending to necessity without losing discernment, the poise of the spirit under the pressure of life."]],"notes":["Visual — Uṣasti's discrimination","<table><tr><td>Offered</td><td>Accepted?</td><td>Reason</td></tr><tr><td>leftover beans</td><td>yes</td><td>survival — \"I would not have lived otherwise\" (necessity overrides purity)</td></tr><tr><td>leftover water</td><td>no</td><td>\"water I can get at will\" — no necessity, so the purity-rule holds</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  A nuanced ethic: rules of purity yield to the necessity of life, but not to mere convenience — discrimination, not rigidity, is the mark of the wise.","•  Even in destitution the knower keeps his discernment and provides for his wife (saving the surplus) — knowledge expressed as steadiness and care, not as display.","•  The narrative dignifies worldly hardship: spiritual stature is shown not by comfort but by clear judgement under pressure."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 11 (1.11)","title":"Uṣasti reveals the deities of the chant","sa":["... प्राण इति होवाच सर्वाणि ह वा इमानि भूतानि प्राणमेवाभिसंविशन्ति","प्राणमभ्युज्जिहते सैषा देवता प्रस्तावमन्वायत्ता ... ॥ ५ ॥","... आदित्य इति होवाच ... साैषा देवतोद्गीथमन्वायत्ता ... ॥ ७ ॥","... अन्नमिति होवाच ... साैषा देवता प्रतिहारमन्वायत्ता ... ॥ ९ ॥"],"words":[["prastotaḥ / udgātaḥ / pratihartaḥ","m. voc.","O Prastotṛ / Udgātṛ / Pratihartṛ (three Sāman priests)"],["prastāvam / udgītham / pratihāram anvāyattā","m. acc. + f. nom.","(the deity) on which the prastāva / udgītha / pratihāra depends"],["prāṇam abhisaṃviśanti, abhyujjihate","3 pl.","(beings) merge into breath, rise up by breath"],["ādityam uccaiḥ santaṃ gāyanti","m. acc. + 3 pl.","sing of the sun as the \"high\" one"],["annam pratiharamāṇāni jīvanti","n. acc. + pres.part. + 3 pl.","live by taking in food"],["katamā sā devatā","interrog.","\"which is that deity?\""],["mūrdhā te vyapatiṣyat","m. nom. + 3 sg. cond.","\"your head would have fallen\""]],"lit":"(yajamāna learns the priest is Uṣasti and engages him to chant.)<br>\"(At the sacrifice, the chanting about to begin, Uṣasti warns each Sāman-priest that to officiate without knowing the deity of his portion is fatal; the patron engages him; the priests ask the deities.) To the Prastotṛ he said: 'Breath (prāṇa) — for all these beings merge into breath and rise up by breath; this is the deity underlying the prastāva.' To the Udgātṛ: 'The Sun (āditya) — for all beings sing of the sun as the one on high; this is the deity underlying the udgītha.' To the Pratihartṛ: 'Food (anna) — for all beings live by taking in food; this is the deity underlying the pratihāra.' (Had they chanted without knowing, their heads would have fallen.)\"","para":"Vindication of khaṇḍa 10's destitute sage. At a sacrifice, Uṣasti stops the three Sāman-priests (Prastotṛ, Udgātṛ, Pratihartṛ), warning that officiating without knowing the presiding deity of one's chant-portion is fatal (\"your head will fall\"). Engaged in their place, he reveals the deities: the prastāva rests on breath, the udgītha on the Sun, the pratihāra on food — each with a reason (all beings merge in breath, sing the sun as highest, live by food). The episode insists: ritual without knowledge of its inner deity is empty and perilous.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","establishes that upāsanā must accompany karma — the priest who chants without knowing the devatā of his portion gains nothing and courts ruin; the three deities (breath, sun, food) are the supports of the three Sāman-divisions."],["Madhva","the presiding deities are forms/agents of Hari; knowing the Lord as the indwelling power of breath, sun and food is what makes the rite effective; ignorance of Him voids it."],["Rangarāmānuja","the deities are the antaryāmin presiding over each chant-portion; valid worship requires knowing the Lord who underlies the ritual act."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","details the three ṛtvij-offices and their chant-portions (prastāva, udgītha, pratihāra), anvāyatta (\"dependent on/presided by\"), and the reasons attached to each deity."],["Sri Aurobindo","the outer rite is animated by inner powers — breath, the sun of truth, the sustaining food-energy; to perform it ignorant of these is to handle live forces blindly, hence the warning."]],"notes":["prastotaḥ — \"prāṇa\" iti hovāca | sarvāṇi ha vā imāni bhūtāni prāṇam evābhisaṃviśanti, prāṇam abhyujjihate | saiṣā devatā prastāvam anvāyattā || 5 ||","udgātaḥ — \"āditya\" iti hovāca | sarvāṇi ha vā imāni bhūtāny ādityam uccaiḥ santaṃ gāyanti | saiṣā devatodgītham anvāyattā || 7 ||","pratihartaḥ — \"annam\" iti hovāca | sarvāṇi ha vā imāni bhūtāny annam eva pratiharamāṇāni jīvanti | saiṣā devatā pratihāram anvāyattā || 9 ||","Visual — the three chant-portions and their deities","<table><tr><td>Priest</td><td>Chant-portion</td><td>Presiding deity</td><td>Reason</td></tr><tr><td>Prastotṛ</td><td>prastāva (prelude)</td><td>breath (prāṇa)</td><td>all beings merge into &amp; rise by breath</td></tr><tr><td>Udgātṛ</td><td>udgītha (the loud song)</td><td>the Sun (āditya)</td><td>all sing the sun as \"the high one\"</td></tr><tr><td>Pratihartṛ</td><td>pratihāra (response)</td><td>food (anna)</td><td>all beings live by taking in food</td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  The governing principle: action without knowledge of its inner ground is hollow — \"the head falls.\" Bring conscious understanding of what each act rests on to your practice, ritual or otherwise.","•  Each deity has a because: breath (all merge in it), sun (all sing it as highest), food (all live by it) — a meditation on the three sustainers of life.","•  The destitute man holds the saving knowledge: status and learning are decoupled — a recurring Chāndogya reversal."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 12 (1.12)","title":"The Udgītha of the dogs","sa":["अथातः शौव उद्गीथस्तद्ध बको दाल्भ्यो ग्लावो वा मैत्रेयः","स्वाध्यायमुद्वव्राज ॥ १ ॥","तस्मै श्वा श्वेतः प्रादुर्बभूव तमन्ये श्वान उपसमेत्योचुरन्नं नो","भगवानागायत्वशनायामवा इति ॥ २ ॥","... ओ३मदा३मों३पिबा३मों३ देवो वरुणः प्रजापतिः सविता२न्नमिहा२हरत्","अन्नपते३ऽन्नमिहा२हरा२हरो३मिति ॥ ५ ॥"],"iast":["athātaḥ śauva udgīthaḥ | tad dha bako dālbhyo glāvo vā maitreyaḥ svādhyāyam udvavrāja || 1 ||","tasmai śvā śvetaḥ prādurbabhūva | tam anye śvāna upasametyocur \"annaṃ no bhagavān āgāyatv, aśanāyāmavā\" iti || 2 ||","te ha yathaivedaṃ bahiṣpavamānena stoṣyamāṇāḥ saṃrabdhāḥ sarpantīty evam āsasṛpuḥ | te ha samupaviśya hiṃ cakruḥ || 4 ||","\"oṃ adāma | oṃ pibāma | oṃ devo varuṇaḥ prajāpatiḥ savitānnam ihāharat | annapate'nnam ihāhara, āhara, oṃ\" iti || 5 ||"],"words":[["śauvaḥ udgīthaḥ","m. nom.","the canine (dogs') Udgītha"],["svādhyāyam udvavrāja","m. acc. + 3 sg. pf.","went out (to a quiet place) for study"],["śvā śvetaḥ prādurbabhūva","m. nom. + 3 sg. pf.","a white dog appeared"],["annaṃ naḥ āgāyatu","n. acc. + impv.","\"sing us food!\""],["aśanāyāmavā","1 pl.","\"we are hungry\""],["bahiṣpavamānena stoṣyamāṇāḥ","n. instr. + fut.part.","(priests) about to chant the bahiṣpavamāna"],["saṃrabdhāḥ sarpanti","m. nom. pl. + 3 pl.","move in a linked file (each holding the one ahead)"],["hiṃ cakruḥ","3 pl. pf.","made the sound \"hiṃ\" (the chant's opening)"],["adāma · pibāma","1 pl. subj.","\"may we eat · may we drink\""],["annapate annam ihāhara","m. voc. + impv.","\"O lord of food, bring food here\""]],"lit":"\"Now the Udgītha of the dogs: Baka Dālbhya — or Glāva Maitreya — went out for (Vedic) study. To him a white dog appeared; other dogs came up and said, 'Sing us food, sir; we are hungry.' He said, 'Come back to me here in the morning.' (Next day) they came creeping in a linked file, just as priests creep linked together when about to chant the bahiṣpavamāna; they sat and made the sound 'hiṃ,' and sang: 'Oṃ, may we eat! Oṃ, may we drink! Oṃ, may the god Varuṇa, Prajāpati, Savitṛ bring food here! O lord of food, bring food here — bring it — Oṃ!'\"","para":"A famously enigmatic, half-satirical passage. A seer at his studies is approached by dogs (led by a white dog) who beg him to \"sing them food,\" then themselves perform a mock-solemn chant — filing in linked together exactly as the priests do for the bahiṣpavamāna stotra, sounding \"hiṃ,\" and singing a hungry petition for food to Varuṇa, Prajāpati, and Savitṛ. Read straight, it parodies chant-for-gain (priests who sing merely \"for food\"); read symbolically (as the tradition does), the \"dogs\" are the vital breaths/senses processing in order, and the chant for \"food\" is the body's deep cry for sustenance and life — Oṃ framing even that.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","takes the passage as genuine (if cryptic) — the dogs symbolise the prāṇas that \"process\" in order; the chant for food is an anna-prārthanā (prayer for sustenance) framed by Oṃ; some read a gentle censure of greed-driven chanting."],["Madhva","reads the white dog and the procession as concealing a teaching about Vāyu/the breaths as the Lord's agents; the chant directs even the hunger of life toward the Lord of food (Hari)."],["Rangarāmānuja","the petition to Varuṇa, Prajāpati, Savitṛ — all presided by the antaryāmin — frames even the body's need within dependence on the Lord who is the true \"lord of food.\""],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","explains the bahiṣpavamāna file (priests moving linked in a row), the hiṃ-kāra, and the elongated chant-syllables; notes the long-standing debate between the literal-satirical and symbolic-prāṇa readings."],["Sri Aurobindo","the \"dogs\" are the lower vital powers that, marshalled and consecrated, turn even their cry for food into a hymn — life's hunger taken up into the sacred sound."]],"notes":["Visual — the dogs' procession and chant","seer at study  →  WHITE DOG appears  →  other dogs: \"sing us food!\"","│  \"come back at dawn\"","dawn: dogs file in LINKED, like priests at the bahiṣpavamāna stotra","│  sit · make \"hiṃ\"","chant:  \"Oṃ may we eat · Oṃ may we drink ·","O Varuṇa, Prajāpati, Savitṛ — bring food! ·","O lord of food, bring it here — Oṃ\"","Practical steps / observations","•  The tradition reads the \"dogs\" as the prāṇas/senses ordered into a procession — a meditation on how even bodily hunger, when ringed by Oṃ, becomes a kind of sacred chant.","•  Taken as satire, it is a caution against chanting merely \"for food\" (spiritual practice reduced to a means of gain) — a self-aware joke embedded in the Udgītha chapter.","•  The \"linked file\" mirrors ritual exactly: a reminder that form without understanding can look identical to the real thing — the chapter's persistent theme."]},{"ref":"Khaṇḍa 13 (1.13)","title":"The mystic stobha-syllables","sa":["अयं वाव लोको हाउकारः वायुर्हाइकारश्चन्द्रमा अथकारः ।","आत्मेहकारोऽग्निरीकारः ॥ १ ॥","आदित्य ऊकारो निहव एकारो विश्वे देवा औहोयिकारः प्रजापतिर्हिंकारः","प्राणः स्वरोऽन्नं या वाग्विराट् ॥ २ ॥","अनिरुक्तस्त्रयोदशः स्तोभः संचरो हुंकारः ॥ ३ ॥","दुग्धेऽस्मै वाग्दोहं यो वाचो दोहोऽन्नवानन्नादो भवति","य एतामेवꣳ साम्नामुपनिषदं वेदोपनिषदं वेदेति ॥ ४ ॥"],"iast":["ayaṃ vāva loko hāukāraḥ | vāyur hāikāraḥ | candramā athakāraḥ | ātmehakāraḥ | agnir īkāraḥ || 1 ||","āditya ūkāraḥ | nihava ekāraḥ | viśve devā auhoyikāraḥ | prajāpatir hiṃkāraḥ | prāṇaḥ svaraḥ | annaṃ yā | vāg virāṭ || 2 ||","aniruktas trayodaśaḥ stobhaḥ saṃcaro huṃkāraḥ || 3 ||","dugdhe'smai vāg dohaṃ yo vāco doho'nnavān annādo bhavati ya etām evaṃ sāmnām upaniṣadaṃ veda, upaniṣadaṃ vedeti || 4 ||"],"words":[["stobha","m.","a meaningless interjected chant-syllable of the Sāman"],["ayaṃ lokaḥ — \"hāu\"","the stobha \"hāu\" = this world",""],["vāyuḥ — \"hāi\"","air = \"hāi\"",""],["candramāḥ — \"atha\"","moon = \"atha\"",""],["ātmā — \"iha\"","the self = \"iha\"",""],["agniḥ — \"ī\"","fire = \"ī\"",""],["ādityaḥ — \"ū\" · nihavaḥ — \"e\"","sun = \"ū\" · the invocation = \"e\"",""],["viśve devāḥ — \"auhoyi\"","the All-gods = \"auhoyi\"",""],["prajāpatiḥ — \"hiṃ\" · prāṇaḥ — svara","Prajāpati = \"hiṃ\" · breath = the tone",""],["annam — \"yā\" · vāk — virāṭ","food = \"yā\" · speech = Virāj",""],["aniruktaḥ trayodaśaḥ stobhaḥ — \"huṃ\"","the undefined 13th stobha = the variable \"huṃ\"",""],["vācaḥ dohaḥ","m. nom.","the \"milk\" (yield) of speech"]],"lit":"\"This world is the syllable hāu; air is hāi; the moon is atha; the self is iha; fire is ī. The sun is ū; the invocation is e; the All-gods are auhoyi; Prajāpati is hiṃ; breath is the tone (svara); food is yā; speech is Virāj. The undefined, thirteenth stobha is the variable sound huṃ. To him who knows this secret (upaniṣad) of the Sāmans, speech yields its milk (its full nourishment); he becomes rich in food and an eater of food — yes, to him who knows this secret.\"","para":"The chapter closes by decoding the stobhas — the seemingly meaningless cry-syllables (hāu, hāi, atha, iha…) interjected in Sāman-singing — as secret names of cosmic realities (worlds, elements, gods, breath, food, speech). The thirteenth, huṃ, is left \"undefined,\" a movable wildcard. Knowing this hidden correspondence is the upaniṣad (secret doctrine) of the Sāmans: it makes \"speech yield its milk\" — the knower becomes abundant in food and able to enjoy it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","the stobhas, though grammatically meaningless, are made vehicles of upāsanā by these correspondences; this is the \"secret of the Sāmans,\" and its fruit (\"speech yields milk,\" abundance of food) caps the prosperity-oriented meditations of the chapter."],["Madhva","the stobhas, rightly known, point to Hari and His vibhūtis (worlds, gods, breath, food); the \"undefined\" huṃ hints at the Lord's incomprehensible fullness."],["Rangarāmānuja","the syllable-correspondences are meditations on the Lord's cosmic forms; knowing them, the worshipper draws the \"milk\" of speech — the Lord's bestowed plenty."],["Gambhīrānanda-tradition","defines stobha (the inserted ritual cries of Sāman-singing), lists the assignments, and explains aniruktа (\"indefinite/unexpressed\") and saṃcara (\"roving/variable\") for the thirteenth, with vāco dohaḥ as \"the milk (yield) of speech.\""],["Sri Aurobindo","even the wordless cries of the chant carry hidden powers of being; the huṃ left free is the unbound creative sound — the seer hears significance where convention hears only noise."]],"notes":["Visual — the stobha → reality code","<table><tr><td>Stobha</td><td>=</td><td>Stobha</td><td>=</td></tr><tr><td>hāu</td><td>this world</td><td>e</td><td>the invocation (nihava)</td></tr><tr><td>hāi</td><td>air</td><td>auhoyi</td><td>the All-gods</td></tr><tr><td>atha</td><td>moon</td><td>hiṃ</td><td>Prajāpati</td></tr><tr><td>iha</td><td>the self (ātman)</td><td>svara (tone)</td><td>breath</td></tr><tr><td>ī</td><td>fire</td><td>yā</td><td>food</td></tr><tr><td>ū</td><td>sun</td><td>vāk</td><td>Virāj</td></tr><tr><td>huṃ</td><td>the undefined 13th stobha (variable, saṃcara)</td><td></td><td></td></tr></table>","Practical steps / observations","•  The teaching is that nothing in the chant is \"mere sound\" — even the interjected cries encode realities. A discipline of treating every syllable as meaning-bearing.","•  The thirteenth, huṃ, deliberately left aniruktа (\"undefined\") and saṃcara (\"moving\"), is a contemplative reminder that some power is unfixed — the open variable behind the fixed set.","•  \"Speech yields its milk to the knower\": the practical fruit is fullness of expression and sustenance — knowing the inner sense makes the chant nourishing, not rote."]}]},{"t":"Prapāṭhaka I · Prapāṭhaka I — thematic outline","u":[{"ref":"Prapāṭhaka I","title":"thematic outline","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["<table><tr><td>Khaṇḍa</td><td>Title</td><td>Type</td><td>Key idea</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Oṃ, essence of essences</td><td>meditation</td><td>Oṃ/Udgītha as the rasa of all; syllable of assent; fulfils desires</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>the chief breath</td><td>meditation/myth</td><td>senses are \"pierced,\" but the mukhya-prāṇa is sinless &amp; unconquerable</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Sun, vyāna, syllables</td><td>meditation</td><td>Udgītha as sun, as held-breath, as ud-gī-tha (breath-speech-food)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>beyond death</td><td>meditation/myth</td><td>gods escape death by entering the imperishable svara (Oṃ)</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Udgītha = Praṇava</td><td>meditation</td><td>one Oṃ in sun &amp; breath; the form of meditation shapes its fruit</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>golden Person in the Sun</td><td>meditation</td><td>cosmic Ṛc/Sāman pairs; the radiant \"Ud,\" risen above all evil</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Person in the eye</td><td>meditation</td><td>the bodily counterpart, one form with the solar Person</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>three masters</td><td>dialogue</td><td>the chain of the Sāman's \"goals\"; Dālbhya halts at heaven</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>ākāśa</td><td>dialogue</td><td>space as origin &amp; end of all — the \"ever-higher,\" endless Udgītha</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Uṣasti &amp; the beans</td><td>narrative</td><td>discrimination in famine; necessity vs. the purity-rule</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>the deities of the chant</td><td>narrative</td><td>breath, sun, food as deities of prastāva/udgītha/pratihāra</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>the dogs' Udgītha</td><td>enigma</td><td>a cryptic/satirical chant for food; the prāṇas in procession</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>the stobha-syllables</td><td>meditation</td><td>the chant's cry-syllables decoded as cosmic realities</td></tr></table>","Arc of the prapāṭhaka: essence (1) → the unconquerable breath (2) → cosmic &amp; bodily seats of Oṃ (3–7) → the dialogue tracing the Sāman to its all-containing ground, ākāśa (8–9) → narratives on the knower's power over the merely ritual (10–12) → the secret of the chant-syllables (13). Throughout, Śaṅkara's distinction holds: these are upāsanās yielding prosperity and a graded path — the ground being prepared for the direct brahma-vidyā of Prapāṭhaka VI."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Notes on this instalment","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["- Sanskrit follows the Sāmaveda (Kauthuma) recension as in sanskritdocuments.org chhaandogya.itx, cross-checked against Śaṅkara's bhāṣya. Numbering is the standard Prapāṭhaka·Khaṇḍa·mantra system (e.g. 1.6.7).","- IAST is given for every khaṇḍa; gloss tables give full pada analysis for the verse/short units and key-term analysis for the long narrative khaṇḍas (8, 10, 11, 12), where exhaustive particle-by-particle glossing would obscure more than it reveals. Long khaṇḍas are quoted by representative passages (marked) with full prose translation.","- The five-commentary digests are this compiler's summaries of each tradition's characteristic reading — Śaṅkara, Madhva, Rangarāmānuja from their Sanskrit works, and the Gambhīrānanda reading-tradition and Sri Aurobindo in paraphrase. They are not verbatim translations of any modern copyrighted edition.","- Commentary set is consistent with the Aitareya instalment (Madhva in place of Sureśvara, who wrote no Chāndogya commentary), giving Advaita (Śaṅkara), Dvaita (Madhva) and Viśiṣṭādvaita (Rangarāmānuja) plus two modern voices.","- Next instalment: Prapāṭhaka II — the Sāma-upāsana (the meditations on the Sāman), appended below this section."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
